WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.291
EDR is not only about detection.

00:00:02.291 --> 00:00:03.752
It also supports response.

00:00:03.752 --> 00:00:08.866
Depending on the tool and permissions, responders may isolate a host from the network,

00:00:08.866 --> 00:00:13.614
kill a process, quarantine a file, collect forensic data, search for related indicators,

00:00:13.614 --> 00:00:15.075
or trigger remediation actions.

00:00:15.075 --> 00:00:18.728
These capabilities can reduce the time between detection and containment.

00:00:18.728 --> 00:00:24.206
If a laptop appears to be spreading malware or connecting to a known command-and-control server,

00:00:24.206 --> 00:00:27.493
isolation can help prevent additional damage while analysts investigate.

00:00:27.493 --> 00:00:29.685
If a suspicious file is found,

00:00:29.685 --> 00:00:32.972
quarantine can reduce exposure while preserving information for review.

00:00:32.972 --> 00:00:34.433
Response actions require judgment.

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:37.355
Isolating a critical server may disrupt business operations.

00:00:37.355 --> 00:00:39.912
Killing a process may destroy useful evidence.

00:00:39.912 --> 00:00:44.660
A good EDR workflow balances speed, evidence preservation, business impact, and risk reduction.
